    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 123.363, 582.03, 185.836, 618.241 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "igerian author Chigozie Obioma’s audacious debut, The Fishermen, captured everyone’s attention"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 33,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 89.995, 482.359, 185.901, 582.636 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "when it made the Man Booker shortlist back in 2015. In it, Obioma elevated the ordinary sibling rivalry between four brothers living in a small town in western Nigeria to the dizzying heights of Greek tragedy. A similar act of transformation can be found in his impressive, epic second novel, the narrative of which follows the (mis)adventures of one Chinonso Solomon Olisa, a humble 26-yearoldpoultryfarmerintheNigerian cityofUmuahia."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 34,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 89.995, 396.943, 185.899, 482.972 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "One day, while driving home from the market where he has purchased some new birds for his ﬂock, Chinoso comes across a woman about to throw herself to her death from a highway bridge. In agitation, he grabs two of the fowl and tosses them over the bridge to demonstrate the gruesomeness of the death that awaits her. The young woman, a trainee pharmacist whose name is Ndali,"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 35,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 194.321, 624.707, 290.183, 668.017 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "is so struck by this intervention that she changes her mind. Chinonso drives home, “uncertain about what he had done, only knowing that he had done somethingout oftheordinary”."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 36,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 194.321, 574.868, 290.225, 625.32 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "Ndali and Chinonso soon fall in love. They want to get married, but her family – who are much wealthier than Chinonso, and are perturbed by his lack of formal education – do everything in their powerto foil thelovers’plans."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 37,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 194.321, 503.68, 290.165, 575.474 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "Thus,whenanoldschoolfriend of Chinoso’s reappears in his life, claiming he’ll help his pal to enrol in a university on Cyprus – he can prove himself worthy of Ndali by earning his degree – Chinonso, “a man of instinct and passion”, rashly decides to sell all his possessions, including his house and farm, tofundtheplan."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 38,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 194.321, 460.975, 290.153, 504.321 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "In The Fishermen, Obioma knitted together Aristotelian tragedy with the oral storytelling traditions of his characters’ cultural heritage, and he takes a similarpatchworkapproachhere."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 39,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 194.328, 396.915, 290.213, 461.623 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "ThenarrativeofAnOrchestraof Minorities owes much to Homer’s Odyssey.Chinonso’sexperiencesin Cyprus are just as challenging as thestrugglesfacedbyhisclassical predecessor during his years of wandering. But Obioma blends these elements of the narrative with Igbo folklore and myth. The"
  }, {
    "type" : "image",
    "pdfua_tag" : "Figure",
    "id" : 6,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 298.608, 405.348, 498.912, 665.652 ],
