    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "UpdatingaclassicObiomablendstheHomerianwithIgbomythJASON KEITH"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 40,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 507.508, 610.444, 603.203, 660.869 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "novel, for example, is narrated by Chinonso’s chi, the guardian spirit who watches over man in Igbo cosmology.It’savoicereminiscent of a Greek chorus: ever-watchful, knowledgeable,butunfortunately unableto intervene."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 41,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 507.508, 503.645, 603.206, 611.05 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "Obioma’s interest in examining the tension between fate and selfdetermination also runs through both his novels. Poor, unfortunate Chinoso finds himself “wronged bymanandhistory”–ﬁrsthefalls foulofamigrationscam,andthen ﬁndshimselffalselyaccusedofattempted rape and murder (a not wholly convincing episode) – as a consequence of which, “a great darkness” enters him, “a crawly, multileggeddarkness,shapedlike a rapidly procreating millipede thatburrowedintohislife”."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 42,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 507.508, 418.229, 603.201, 504.251 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "Where brother turning on brother in The Fishermen could be read as an allegory of post-independence Nigeria, the parable here speaks to broader concerns about the cruelties of the contemporary world; about what the powerful do to the weak, and how a man can be expected to endure only so much before he begins to want to inﬂict the same torments onthose whohavehurthim."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 43,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 507.508, 396.873, 603.139, 418.835 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "Timely, portentous and powerful, Obioma’s second novel conﬁrmshis remarkabletalent."
  }, {
